  "metric_rationales": {
    "a": "Abstractness = interfaces / exported types. A stable module that is all concrete types is hard to change without breaking its dependents; interfaces give them something to hold on to.",
    "ca": "Afferent coupling: how many other modules import this one. High Ca means many things rely on it, so changing it is risky.",
    "cc": "Cyclomatic complexity: the number of independent paths through a function. The higher it is, the more tests it needs and the harder it is to read.",
    "ce": "Efferent coupling: how many modules this one imports. High Ce means it can break when any of them changes.",
    "crap": "CRAP = CC^2 * (1 - coverage)^3 + CC. It combines complexity with missing test coverage: a complex function with weak tests is the riskiest to change.",
    "d": "Distance from the main sequence = |A + I - 1|. 0 means stability and abstraction are in balance; near 1 means the zone of pain (stable and concrete) or the zone of uselessness (abstract and unstable).",
    "i": "Instability = Ce / (Ca + Ce), from 0 (everything depends on it, it depends on nothing) to 1 (it depends on others, nothing depends on it). Stable modules should be hard to break; unstable ones are cheap to change."
  }
